  Sorry.  That wasn't my intent.  I just get a large case of MEGOcity
whenever the talk turns to obscure linguistic points.  I have the same
attitude towards lojban that Rabbi Hillel (ca 2000 years ago) had towards
Jewish Law (halachah).  To find out what the actual practice of the law is,
turn to see what the Jewish people are doing (ex. Babylonion Talmu Pesachim
66a).  To find out what the definition of lojbanic grammar/words are, see
how it's actually being used.  And one can't do that if it's not actually
being used.  I.e., less talk /about/ lojban , more talk /in/ lojban. (pe'i
lo nu tavla fi la lojban kei lo nu tavla ba'e fo  la lojban kei lo ka vajni
cu na zbasu). I recognize that not everyone treats lojban as a living
language, however.
                      --gejyspa



